    "rationale": "The only cell on this path attributed to uart_rx.v is stage 0, the launch flop _124902_ (DFF_X1, 0.080 ns), whose src line (170) is \"bit_sample <= rxd_reg;\" inside p_bit_sample. That stage cost is just clock-to-Q of the launching register; it is not reducible from within this module, and there is no combinational logic between that flop and the next register that is attributed to uart_rx.v at all. bit_sample is a purely internal reg (no output port), so the 12 unattributed cells that follow (NOR4\u2192NAND4\u2192OAI22\u2192NOR2\u2192AOI211\u2192AND3\u2192AND3\u2192NAND3\u2192XOR2\u2192XOR2\u2192XOR2\u2192NOR3, summing to 0.860 ns of the 0.940 ns path) cannot be logic fed directly by an internal signal of this module reaching into a different clock-domain module's flop with only 2 sequential cells total on the path (i.e., pure combinational glue). The cell shapes (three cascaded XOR2 stages typical of Gray-code encode/compare, followed by wide AOI/OAI/NOR/NAND gates typical of a full-flag equality/priority network) match exactly what one expects inside wptr_full.v (write-pointer full-flag generation), which is also where the endpoint register _125646_ (DFFR_X1) physically lives per its src annotation. Since none of that combinational depth is visible or editable in uart_rx.v, RETIME is unavailable (no register sits inside the dominant chain that I can move), RESTRUCTURE is unavailable (the shallow logic I can see, the FSM next-state mux and uart_rx_valid/break expressions, is not on this path and is already minimal), and PIPELINE would require inserting a register into combinational logic I cannot see or safely rebalance against its reconvergent fan-in (the full-flag compare network), risking a functional break in the async-fifo full/empty protocol. Therefore no safe fix exists within the uart_rx.v scope shown.",
